.TH "darwin.conf" "5" "Slurm Configuration File" "Slurm 26.11" "Slurm Configuration File"

.SH "NAME"
darwin.conf \- Slurm configuration file for native macOS task controls

.SH "DESCRIPTION"
\fBdarwin.conf\fR defines administrator policies for \fBtask/darwin\fR.
It must be in the same directory as the selected \fBslurm.conf\fR.
Configless nodes receive it with the other Slurm configuration files.
Selecting \fBTaskPlugin=task/darwin\fR requires a readable regular file; an empty or
comments-only file leaves all native policies disabled.
Use \fBTaskPlugin=task/none\fR when these task controls are not wanted.
.LP
Parameter names are case insensitive. Text following a "#" is a comment
through the end of the line. Numeric values are unsigned integers without
suffixes. One MiB is 1,048,576 bytes. Restart Slurm daemons after changing this
file; existing steps retain their previously loaded policy.
Keep the file and its containing directory administrator controlled.
.LP
These policies are independent of job CPU and memory allocations and the job
time limit. They do not provide hard aggregate CPU bandwidth, a shared
physical-memory budget, CPU pinning or complete device isolation.
Missing configuration, invalid values and unavailable requested controls
cause initialization or task launch to fail.

.SH "PARAMETERS"
.TP
\fBPerProcessCPUTimeSeconds\fR
Hard \fBRLIMIT_CPU\fR ceiling on each process's accumulated CPU seconds,
summed over its threads. The default is 0 (disabled).
A nonzero value must fit below the platform's \fBRLIM_INFINITY\fR value.
The hard limit is lowered and the soft limit is lowered only as needed to
fit within it; stricter existing values are preserved.
The ceiling is inherited across fork and exec, but CPU usage is counted
separately for each process. This is not a CPU rate or a shared job-time budget.
It applies to task prologs and task epilogs as well as the task executable.
.IP

.TP
\fBPerProcessAddressSpaceMiB\fR
Hard \fBRLIMIT_AS\fR ceiling on each process's virtual address space, in MiB.
The default is 0 (disabled).
The converted byte value must fit below the platform's \fBRLIM_INFINITY\fR
value. Stricter existing hard and soft limits are preserved, and the ceiling
is inherited across fork and exec.
Task prologs and task epilogs receive the same ceiling.
Reserved mappings count even when they consume little physical memory.
Do not derive this setting directly from a job's physical-memory request.
Darwin aliases \fBRLIMIT_RSS\fR to \fBRLIMIT_AS\fR; Slurm exposes AS alone
and rejects an explicit RSS propagation request on Darwin.
.IP

.SH "LIMITATIONS"
\fBtask/darwin\fR rejects explicit CPU/NUMA binding masks, CPU/GPU frequency
controls, \fBTaskPluginParam=OOMKillStep\fR and a running job's aggregate
memory-limit reduction. Attaching an external PID to a Slurm extern step is
unsupported on Darwin, even with native policies disabled. Launch policies
cannot be applied to already running processes. Normal task children and their
observed descendants remain eligible for process tracking.
.LP
\fBJobAcctGatherParams=OverMemoryKill\fR is a separate sampled policy.
It may cancel a job after observing excess usage, but cannot prevent memory
allocation between samples.

.SH "EXAMPLE"
In \fBslurm.conf\fR:
.nf
TaskPlugin=task/darwin
.fi
.LP
In \fBdarwin.conf\fR:
.nf
PerProcessCPUTimeSeconds=3600
PerProcessAddressSpaceMiB=0
.fi
.LP
This example limits each process to 3600 CPU seconds. The address-space limit
remains disabled; a job's aggregate CPU use is not limited to 3600 seconds.

